<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swiss peasant war of 1653</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1653">1653</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Swiss peasant war of 1653 was a revolt of the rural subjects of Lucerne, Bern, Solothurn and Basel against their patrician city governments, set off by a devaluation of the currency and falling prices after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. Led by Niklaus Leuenberger, the peasants formed the League of Huttwil, but the forces of the cities defeated them at Wohlenschwil and Herzogenbuchsee, and Leuenberger and other leaders were executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swiss-peasant-war-of-1653.jpg" alt="Swiss peasant war of 1653" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lupo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Swiss_Peasant_War_of_1653.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the city cantons of the Old Swiss Confederacy the city councils ruled the countryside, and since the 15th century power had gathered in a few urban families who came to treat their offices as hereditary. Swiss peasants had profited from high export prices during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, many taking on mortgages, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> the southern German economy recovered and prices fell. The cities, short of the pensions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had paid for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> regiments, raised taxes and minted copper Batzen at the face value of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, which lost purchasing power. Earlier tax revolts had broken out in Lucerne, Bern and Zürich.</p><p>In December 1652 Bern devalued its Batzen by 50% and allowed only three days to exchange them at the old rate, so that most country people lost half their savings. In the Entlebuch valley, led by Hans Emmenegger and Christian Schybi, an illegal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a> at Heiligkreuz decided on 10 February 1653 to withhold all taxes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Most rural districts of Lucerne joined the Entlebuch at Wolhusen on 26 February, and the Bernese Emmental followed. Compromises mediated by other cantons failed, and on 14 May the peasants signed a written treaty at Huttwil in the style of the old Bundesbriefe. The league claimed to be a political entity equal to the cities, appealed to the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tell">William Tell</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stans-agreement">Stanser Verkommnis</a> of 1481, and united Catholic and Protestant peasants. With 16,000 men they besieged Bern and Lucerne, and on 28 May Bern accepted the peace on the Murifeld.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Tagsatzung</a> then sent an army of some 8,000 men from Zürich under Conrad Werdmüller, who refused to recognise the Murifeld treaty. On 3 June 1653 about 24,000 poorly armed peasants without artillery were defeated at Wohlenschwil, and the peace of Mellingen annulled the league. Bernese troops under Sigmund von Erlach plundered villages and won at Herzogenbuchsee on 7 June, and the troops of Sebastian Peregrin Zwyer held the Entlebuch by 20 June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Bern rejected the amnesty of Mellingen, disarmed the countryside and imposed heavy fines. It handed down 23 death sentences, Lucerne eight and Basel seven; Schybi was executed at Sursee on 9 July. Yet the cities, aware how narrowly they had escaped, lowered taxes and curbed their sheriffs, and the historian Suter concludes that the war checked absolutism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. The authorities banned the peasants' flags, clubs and songs, but nineteenth-century democrats such as the illustrator Martin Disteli took up the war as a forerunner of their own struggle, and monuments to its leaders followed from 1903.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swiss peasant war of 1653” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It led the Catholic cantons during the Reformation, hosted the papal nuncio from 1586, and with its allies crushed the great <b>Peasants' War</b> of 1653.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-lucerne">Canton of Lucerne</a></li></ul></section>
